id=abk_runtime_tunables
name=ABK 5.15 Runtime Tunables
version=v0.11.0
versionCode=15
author=FanZiyun
description=Drives the ABK 5.15 kernel grafts that are otherwise inert on device: keeps the zram algorithm policy in force at every boot (primary lz4kd, secondary zstd, locked in the kernel by Batch 11 and re-asserted here on older kernels), preserves or establishes the zram writeback backing device instead of trading it away, drives the writeback sweep itself when zram.writeback.trigger is set (the kernel only moves a page when userspace asks, and nothing else on this ROM asks), adds the kernel-domain SELinux rules that path needs under Enforcing (Batch 18; one per backing-file type, because the ROM's extm backs its loop device with extm_data_file rather than zram_data_file), runs age-based recompression sweeps through the async worker, capped per pass by zram.recomp.max_pages (Batch 24 grafts the max_pages parameter; an older kernel ignores it and the sweep stays unbounded), follows each sweep with one gated zsmalloc compaction pass, records who owns CPU frequency at boot (a vendor FAS/WALT governor must keep it, since Batch 10-5 ships the smart-freq floor off), and reports or tunes the remaining runtime knobs (MGLRU, THP, swappiness, abk_sf, dynamic readahead, cgroup-v1 proactive reclaim, and per-cgroup pressure (PSI) accounting switched off for the hundreds of groups nobody reads through the Batch 21 cgroup.pressure node, which makes it a policy pass plus a two-boot measurement rather than a new graft). Batch 27 adds a cold-launch instrument (bin/abk_launch_bench.sh, also `action.sh launch`) that measures am start -W per app and says which layer the launch window was bound by -- the placer, the cpuset, or storage -- refusing to report a median it did not take and refusing to measure a dozing device, because a screen-off launch never makes the app the top app.